<subsection xml:id="subsec-triple-integrals-evaluation">
  <title>Evaluating Triple Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Triple integrals are evaluated by reducing them to three successive
      one-variable integrals, exactly as for double integrals.
      For a chosen order of integration, the domain must be expressible as a
      <em>normal domain</em>: the bounds of an inner variable may depend on the
      outer variables that are already fixed, but not on variables that are
      integrated earlier.
      We treat Cartesian and cylindrical coordinates in turn.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Normal domains in 3D ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-normal-domain-3d">
    <title>Normal Domains in <m>\R^3</m></title>
    <p>
      For the integration order <m>dz\,dy\,dx</m>, a <em>normal domain</em> in
      three dimensions is a spatial region described by inequalities of the form:
      <md>
        <mrow>a \amp\leq x \leq b,</mrow>
        <mrow>y_l(x) \amp\leq y \leq y_u(x),</mrow>
        <mrow>z_l(x,y) \amp\leq z \leq z_u(x,y).</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The structure is hierarchical:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>outer</em> bounds (for <m>x</m>) are constants.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>middle</em> bounds (for <m>y</m>) may depend on <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>inner</em> bounds (for <m>z</m>) may depend on both
          <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Other variable orderings, such as <m>dx\,dz\,dy</m>, are equally valid.
      The useful order is the one whose bounds describe the geometry most simply.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="ex-cylinder-normal-domain">
    <title>Cylinder as a Normal Domain</title>
    <p>
      Show that a solid cylinder of radius <m>R</m> and height <m>h</m>,
      with its base centred at the origin, is a normal domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Project the cylinder onto the <m>xy</m>-plane: the cross-section is the
      disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \leq R^2</m>.  Describing this disk as a Type I planar
      normal domain gives <m>-R \leq x \leq R</m> and
      <m>-\sqrt{R^2 - x^2} \leq y \leq \sqrt{R^2 - x^2}</m>.
      The height runs from <m>0</m> to <m>h</m>.  Combining:
      <md>
        <mrow>-R \amp\leq x \leq R,</mrow>
        <mrow>-\sqrt{R^2-x^2} \amp\leq y \leq \sqrt{R^2-x^2},</mrow>
        <mrow>0 \amp\leq z \leq h.</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-normal-domain">
      <caption>
        A vertical cylinder as a normal domain. For each fixed <m>x</m>, the
        variable <m>y</m> runs through the chord of the disk, and <m>z</m> runs
        from the base <m>0</m> to the height <m>h</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-cylinder-normal-domain" width="55%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0,
            x={({cos(210)*0.65cm},{sin(210)*0.65cm})},
            y={({cos(330)*0.65cm},{sin(330)*0.65cm})},
            z={(0,0.9cm)}, font=\small]
            \def\R{1.35}
            \def\H{2.0}
            \draw[->,thick] (-1.8,0,0) -- (2.0,0,0) node[below right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,-1.7,0) -- (0,1.9,0) node[right] {$y$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.45) node[above] {$z$};
            \fill[blue!12, opacity=0.65] (0,0,0) ellipse ({\R} and {0.45});
            \fill[blue!20, opacity=0.55] (0,0,\H) ellipse ({\R} and {0.45});
            \draw[blue!65!black, thick] (0,0,0) ellipse ({\R} and {0.45});
            \draw[blue!65!black, thick] (0,0,\H) ellipse ({\R} and {0.45});
            \draw[blue!65!black, thick] (-\R,0,0) -- (-\R,0,\H);
            \draw[blue!65!black, thick] (\R,0,0) -- (\R,0,\H);
            \draw[dashed, blue!65!black] (0,-\R,0) -- (0,-\R,\H);
            \draw[dashed, blue!65!black] (0,\R,0) -- (0,\R,\H);
            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (-0.55,-1.18,0) -- (-0.55,1.18,0);
            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (-0.55,-1.18,\H) -- (-0.55,1.18,\H);
            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (-0.55,-1.18,0) -- (-0.55,-1.18,\H);
            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (-0.55,1.18,0) -- (-0.55,1.18,\H);
            \node[red!70!black] at (-0.75,1.45,1.0) {$x=\text{const.}$};
            \node[blue!70!black] at (1.35,0.55,\H) {$z=h$};
            \node[below] at (-\R,0,0) {$-R$};
            \node[below] at (\R,0,0) {$R$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Cartesian theorem ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-triple-integral-cartesian">
    <title>Triple Integrals in Cartesian Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a normal domain <m>V</m> with
        <m>a \leq x \leq b</m>,
        <m>y_l(x) \leq y \leq y_u(x)</m>,
        <m>z_l(x,y) \leq z \leq z_u(x,y)</m>:
        <me>
          \iiint\limits_V f(x,y,z)\,dV
          = \int_a^b \int_{y_l(x)}^{y_u(x)} \int_{z_l(x,y)}^{z_u(x,y)}
            f(x,y,z)\,dz\,dy\,dx.
        </me>
        The innermost integral (in <m>z</m>) is computed first, treating
        <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as parameters.
        The middle integral (in <m>y</m>) is then computed, treating <m>x</m>
        as a parameter.
        The outermost integral (in <m>x</m>) is an ordinary definite integral.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Cartesian worked examples ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-triple-integral-sine">
    <title>Triple Integral with Trigonometric Integrand</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x,y,z) = yz\sin x</m> and
        <me>
          V =
          \{(x,y,z) \in \R^3 \mid
          0 \leq x \leq \tfrac{\pi}{2},\;
          0 \leq y \leq 1,\;
          y^2 \leq z \leq y\}.
        </me>
        Compute
        <me>
          \iiint\limits_V yz\sin x\,dV.
        </me>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-triple-integral-sine-domain">
        <caption>
          The domain is an extrusion in the <m>x</m>-direction of the region
          between <m>z=y^2</m> and <m>z=y</m> for <m>0\leq y\leq 1</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-triple-integral-sine-domain" width="55%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15,
              x={({cos(210)*0.75cm},{sin(210)*0.75cm})},
              y={(1cm,0cm)},
              z={(0,1cm)}, font=\small]
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.0,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,1.45,0) node[right] {$y$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1.35) node[above] {$z$};
              \fill[blue!18, opacity=0.75]
                (0,0,0) -- (0,0.25,0.25) -- (0,0.5,0.5) -- (0,0.75,0.75)
                -- (0,1,1) -- (1.65,1,1) -- (1.65,0.75,0.75)
                -- (1.65,0.5,0.5) -- (1.65,0.25,0.25) -- (1.65,0,0)
                -- cycle;
              \fill[blue!10, opacity=0.8]
                (0,0,0) -- (0,0.25,0.0625) -- (0,0.5,0.25)
                -- (0,0.75,0.5625) -- (0,1,1) -- (1.65,1,1)
                -- (1.65,0.75,0.5625) -- (1.65,0.5,0.25)
                -- (1.65,0.25,0.0625) -- (1.65,0,0) -- cycle;
              \draw[blue!70!black, thick]
                plot[domain=0:1, samples=40] (0,\x,\x)
                node[right] {$z=y$};
              \draw[blue!70!black, thick]
                plot[domain=0:1, samples=40] (0,\x,{\x*\x})
                node[right] {$z=y^2$};
              \draw[blue!70!black, thick]
                plot[domain=0:1, samples=40] (1.65,\x,\x);
              \draw[blue!70!black, thick]
                plot[domain=0:1, samples=40] (1.65,\x,{\x*\x});
              \draw[blue!70!black] (0,0,0) -- (1.65,0,0);
              \draw[blue!70!black] (0,1,1) -- (1.65,1,1);
              \node[below] at (1.65,0,0) {$\pi/2$};
              \node at (0.95,0.55,0.42) {$V$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Note that for <m>y \in [0,1]</m> we have <m>y^2 \leq y</m>, so the
        <m>z</m>-bounds are valid.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Inner integral (in <m>z</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{y^2}^{y} yz\sin x\,dz
            \amp= y\sin x\left[\tfrac{1}{2}z^2\right]_{y^2}^{y}
            = \tfrac{1}{2}y\sin x\,(y^2 - y^4).
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Middle integral (in <m>y</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^1 \tfrac{1}{2}y\sin x\,(y^2 - y^4)\,dy
            \amp= \tfrac{\sin x}{2}\left[\tfrac{y^4}{4} - \tfrac{y^6}{6}\right]_0^1
            = \tfrac{\sin x}{2}\!\left(\tfrac{1}{4} - \tfrac{1}{6}\right)
            = \tfrac{\sin x}{24}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Outer integral (in <m>x</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^{\pi/2} \tfrac{\sin x}{24}\,dx
            \amp= \tfrac{1}{24}\left[-\cos x\right]_0^{\pi/2}
            = \tfrac{1}{24}(0 - (-1)) = \tfrac{1}{24}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-triple-integral-yez">
    <title>Triple Integral with Exponential Integrand</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x,y,z) = ye^z</m> and
        <me>
          V =
          \{(x,y,z)\in\R^3 \mid
          1 \leq x \leq 2,\;
          0 \leq y \leq x,\;
          0 \leq z \leq x-y\}.
        </me>
        Compute
        <me>
          \iiint\limits_V ye^z\,dV.
        </me>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-triple-integral-yez-domain">
        <caption>
          The domain lies between the planes <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=x-y</m>, with
          <m>1\leq x\leq 2</m> and <m>0\leq y\leq x</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-triple-integral-yez-domain" width="55%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95,
              x={({cos(210)*0.75cm},{sin(210)*0.75cm})},
              y={(1cm,0cm)},
              z={(0,1cm)}, font=\small]
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.55,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.4,0) node[right] {$y$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.35) node[above] {$z$};
              \fill[green!16, opacity=0.75]
                (1,0,0) -- (2,0,0) -- (2,2,0) -- (1,1,0) -- cycle;
              \fill[green!28, opacity=0.65]
                (1,0,1) -- (2,0,2) -- (2,2,0) -- (1,1,0) -- cycle;
              \draw[green!55!black, thick]
                (1,0,0) -- (2,0,0) -- (2,2,0) -- (1,1,0) -- cycle;
              \draw[green!65!black, thick]
                (1,0,1) -- (2,0,2) -- (2,2,0) -- (1,1,0) -- cycle;
              \draw[green!55!black] (1,0,0) -- (1,0,1);
              \draw[green!55!black] (2,0,0) -- (2,0,2);
              \node[green!55!black] at (1.55,0.65,0.85) {$z=x-y$};
              \node at (1.55,1.0,0.25) {$V$};
              \node[below] at (1,0,0) {$1$};
              \node[below] at (2,0,0) {$2$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Inner integral (in <m>z</m>):</em>
        <me>
          \int_0^{x-y} ye^z\,dz = y\left[e^z\right]_0^{x-y} = y(e^{x-y} - 1).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Middle integral (in <m>y</m>):</em>
        Split into two parts:
        <me>
          \int_0^x y(e^{x-y} - 1)\,dy
          = \int_0^x ye^{x-y}\,dy - \int_0^x y\,dy.
        </me>
        For the first part, integrate by parts with <m>u = y</m>,
        <m>v' = e^{x-y}</m> (so <m>v = -e^{x-y}</m>):
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^x ye^{x-y}\,dy
            \amp= \left[-ye^{x-y}\right]_0^x + \int_0^x e^{x-y}\,dy
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -x + \left[-e^{x-y}\right]_0^x
            = -x - 1 + e^x.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The second part: <m>\int_0^x y\,dy = \tfrac{x^2}{2}</m>.
        Combining:
        <me>
          \int_0^x y(e^{x-y}-1)\,dy = e^x - x - 1 - \tfrac{x^2}{2}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Outer integral (in <m>x</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_1^2\!\left(e^x - x - 1 - \tfrac{x^2}{2}\right)dx
            \amp= \left[e^x - \tfrac{x^2}{2} - x - \tfrac{x^3}{6}\right]_1^2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \left(e^2 - 2 - 2 - \tfrac{4}{3}\right)
                 - \left(e - \tfrac{1}{2} - 1 - \tfrac{1}{6}\right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= e^2 - e - \tfrac{11}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Cylindrical coordinates ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-cylindrical-coordinates">
    <title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
    <p>
      For spatial domains with <em>rotational symmetry about the
      <m>z</m>-axis</em>, the computation simplifies greatly in
      <term>cylindrical coordinates</term>.
      A point <m>P = (x,y,z)</m> is described by:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>(r,\varphi)</m>: polar coordinates of the orthogonal projection
          of <m>P</m> into the <m>xy</m>-plane, with
          <m>x = r\cos\varphi</m> and <m>y = r\sin\varphi</m>, <m>r \geq 0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>z</m>: the height coordinate, unchanged.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <em>volume element</em> in cylindrical coordinates is
      <me>
        dV = r\,dr\,d\varphi\,dz.
      </me>
      The factor <m>r</m> is the Jacobian of the transformation from
      <m>(x,y,z)</m> to <m>(r,\varphi,z)</m>, and arises for the same reason
      as in polar coordinates for double integrals
      (see <xref ref="rem-polar-domain"/>).
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-coordinates">
      <caption>
        Cylindrical coordinates <m>(r,\varphi,z)</m>.
        The volume element (shaded) has dimensions <m>dr</m>,
        <m>r\,d\varphi</m> (arc length), and <m>dz</m>,
        giving volume <m>dV = r\,dr\,d\varphi\,dz</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-cylindrical-coordinates" width="55%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1,
            x={({cos(210)*0.6cm},{sin(210)*0.6cm})},
            y={({cos(330)*0.6cm},{sin(330)*0.6cm})},
            z={(0,0.9cm)}]
            % Axes
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.8,0,0) node[below right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
            % A point P at r=1.6, phi=40deg, z=1.5
            \def\rr{1.6}
            \def\phiP{40}
            \def\zP{1.5}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\rr*cos(\phiP)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\rr*sin(\phiP)}
            % Projection on xy-plane
            \draw[dashed,gray] (0,0,0) -- (\px,\py,0);
            \draw[dashed,gray] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,\zP);
            % Point
            \fill (\px,\py,\zP) circle (1.5pt)
              node[above right] {$P=(r,\varphi,z)$};
            % r label
            \node[below, gray] at (0.5*\px, 0.5*\py, 0) {$r$};
            % phi arc
            \draw[->,gray] (0.6,0,0)
              arc[start angle=0, end angle=\phiP, radius=0.6]
              node[midway, below right] {$\varphi$};
            % z label
            \draw[&lt;-&gt;,gray] (\px+0.15,\py,0) -- (\px+0.15,\py,\zP)
              node[midway,right] {$z$};
            % Volume element (schematic box at r=1.0, phi=20deg, z=0.7)
            \def\ri{0.9}
            \def\ro{1.2}
            \def\phiA{20}
            \def\phiB{45}
            \def\zA{0.6}
            \def\zB{1.1}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\axA}{\ri*cos(\phiA)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ayA}{\ri*sin(\phiA)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\axB}{\ro*cos(\phiA)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\ayB}{\ro*sin(\phiA)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\bxA}{\ri*cos(\phiB)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\byA}{\ri*sin(\phiB)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\bxB}{\ro*cos(\phiB)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\byB}{\ro*sin(\phiB)}
            % Bottom face
            \fill[blue!15, opacity=0.7]
              (\axA,\ayA,\zA) -- (\axB,\ayB,\zA)
              -- (\bxB,\byB,\zA) -- (\bxA,\byA,\zA) -- cycle;
            % Top face
            \fill[blue!25, opacity=0.7]
              (\axA,\ayA,\zB) -- (\axB,\ayB,\zB)
              -- (\bxB,\byB,\zB) -- (\bxA,\byA,\zB) -- cycle;
            % Side faces
            \fill[blue!10, opacity=0.5]
              (\axA,\ayA,\zA) -- (\axA,\ayA,\zB)
              -- (\axB,\ayB,\zB) -- (\axB,\ayB,\zA) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!10, opacity=0.5]
              (\bxA,\byA,\zA) -- (\bxA,\byA,\zB)
              -- (\bxB,\byB,\zB) -- (\bxB,\byB,\zA) -- cycle;
            % Labels on element
            \node[right, font=\footnotesize, blue!70!black]
              at (\axB,\ayB,{0.5*(\zA+\zB)}) {$dr$};
            \node[below, font=\footnotesize, blue!70!black]
              at ({0.5*(\axA+\bxA)},{0.5*(\ayA+\byA)},\zA) {$r\,d\varphi$};
            \node[right, font=\footnotesize, blue!70!black]
              at (\bxB,\byB,{0.5*(\zA+\zB)}) {$dz$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-triple-integral-cylindrical">
    <title>Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a normal domain <m>V</m> described in cylindrical coordinates by
        <m>r_0 \leq r \leq r_1</m>,
        <m>f_i(r) \leq \varphi \leq f_a(r)</m>,
        <m>z_l(r,\varphi) \leq z \leq z_u(r,\varphi)</m>:
        <me>
          \iiint\limits_V f(x,y,z)\,dV
          = \int_{r_0}^{r_1}
            \int_{f_i(r)}^{f_a(r)}
            \int_{z_l(r,\varphi)}^{z_u(r,\varphi)}
            f(r\cos\varphi,\,r\sin\varphi,\,z)\cdot r
            \,dz\,d\varphi\,dr.
        </me>
        The Jacobian factor <m>r</m> must always be included. As in Cartesian
        coordinates, other orders of integration are possible if they describe
        the same solid more simply.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Cylindrical examples ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-cylinder-moment-inertia">
    <title>Moment of Inertia of a Solid Cylinder</title>
    <p>
      Consider a solid cylinder with radius <m>R</m>, height <m>h</m>
      (centred at the origin, so <m>-h/2 \leq z \leq h/2</m>), and
      constant mass density <m>\rho</m>.
      The total mass is <m>m = \rho\operatorname{vol}(Z) = \rho\pi R^2 h</m>.
      Compute the moment of inertia <m>J_z</m> about the <m>z</m>-axis.
    </p>
    <p>
      In cylindrical coordinates, the cylinder is
      <m>0 \leq r \leq R</m>,
      <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq 2\pi</m>,
      <m>-h/2 \leq z \leq h/2</m>.
      The squared distance from the <m>z</m>-axis is
      <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          J_z \amp= \iiint\limits_Z (x^2+y^2)\,\rho\,dV
          = \rho\int_0^R\int_0^{2\pi}\int_{-h/2}^{h/2}
            r^2 \cdot r\,dz\,d\varphi\,dr
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= \rho\int_0^R\int_0^{2\pi} r^3 h\,d\varphi\,dr
          = \rho\cdot 2\pi h\int_0^R r^3\,dr
          = 2\pi\rho h\cdot\frac{R^4}{4}
          = \frac{\pi\rho h R^4}{2}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Since <m>m = \rho\pi R^2 h</m>:
      <me>
        J_z = \frac{1}{2}mR^2.
      </me>
      This classical result states that the moment of inertia of a cylinder
      about its axis of symmetry equals half the product of its mass and the
      square of its radius.
    </p>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-halfball-geometric-center">
    <title>Geometric Center of a Half-Ball</title>
    <p>
      Find the geometric center of the upper half-ball of radius <m>R</m>:
      <m>V = \{(x,y,z) \mid x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \leq R^2,\; z \geq 0\}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      By symmetry, <m>x_S = y_S = 0</m>.
      In cylindrical coordinates, the half-ball is bounded by
      <m>0 \leq r \leq R</m>,
      <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq 2\pi</m>,
      <m>0 \leq z \leq \sqrt{R^2 - r^2}</m>.
      The volume is <m>\operatorname{vol}(V) = \tfrac{2}{3}\pi R^3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em><m>z</m>-coordinate of the centroid:</em>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \iiint_V z\,dV
          \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^R\int_0^{\sqrt{R^2-r^2}}
            z\cdot r\,dz\,dr\,d\varphi
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= 2\pi\int_0^R r\left[\frac{z^2}{2}\right]_0^{\sqrt{R^2-r^2}}dr
          = 2\pi\int_0^R \frac{r(R^2-r^2)}{2}\,dr
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= \pi\int_0^R (R^2 r - r^3)\,dr
          = \pi\left[\frac{R^2 r^2}{2} - \frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^R
          = \pi\left(\frac{R^4}{2} - \frac{R^4}{4}\right)
          = \frac{\pi R^4}{4}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Therefore
      <me>
        z_S = \frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(V)}\iiint_V z\,dV
            = \frac{1}{\tfrac{2}{3}\pi R^3}\cdot\frac{\pi R^4}{4}
            = \frac{3}{8}R.
      </me>
      The geometric center of the half-ball is at
      <m>\bigl(0,\,0,\,\tfrac{3}{8}R\bigr)</m>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-triple-integrals-evaluation-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-triple-integral-cylindrical-jacobian">
      <title>Cylindrical Jacobian Factor</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which extra factor appears when converting a triple integral to cylindrical coordinates?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\rho</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. That symbol is often used for density, not for the cylindrical Jacobian here.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>r</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. In cylindrical coordinates, <m>dV=r\,dr\,d\varphi\,dz</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>r^2</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The Jacobian contributes only one factor <m>r</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\sin\varphi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The cylindrical Jacobian is not trigonometric.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-triple-integral-cylinder-z">
      <title>Compute a Cylindrical Triple Integral</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute
          <me>
            \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^1\int_0^2 z\,r\,dz\,dr\,d\varphi.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First integrate with respect to <m>z</m>:
          <me>
            \int_0^2 z\,dz = \left[\frac{z^2}{2}\right]_0^2 = 2.
          </me>
          Then
          <me>
            \int_0^1 2r\,dr = \left[r^2\right]_0^1 = 1.
          </me>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \int_0^{2\pi} 1\,d\varphi = 2\pi.
          </me>
          Hence the value of the integral is <m>2\pi</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
